course: dictionary
lesson: 7970
title: Reckon, a word with many jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Which bits do you reckon they picked?» Alfie grins: «The shouty ones.» Here reckon means think or believe. The question asks what someone thinks, not what someone counts, so the context tells you which meaning fits.
explain: Reckon is an informal word, common in the UK, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland. People use it when they give an opinion: «I reckon it'll rain.»
explain: Reckon can also mean guess a number. «How many do you reckon are in the jar?» You are not sure of the exact answer, so you estimate it. Ask yourself whether the sentence wants an opinion or a rough number.
explain: Two little phrases change the meaning. Reckon on means expect or rely on something. Reckon with means you have to deal with someone or something, as in «a force to be reckoned with».
fact: Reckon means to think or believe, or to have an opinion.
fact: Reckon can also mean to guess or estimate a number.
fact: Reckon on means to expect or rely on something.
fact: Reckon with means to have to deal with someone or something.
fact: «A force to be reckoned with» means someone strong or clever who must be taken seriously.
exercise: «Which bits do you reckon they picked?» In this sentence, reckon means | To pay for something | To run very fast | To be sad | *To think or believe
exercise: «How many do you reckon are in the jar?» What does reckon mean here? | To sleep | *To estimate a number | To paint a picture | To shout loudly
exercise: Mum says, «We can reckon on the bus being late.» What does reckon on mean? | To tell a joke | To shout | To count coins | *To expect or rely on
test: Tom says, «I reckon the match will be a draw.» What does reckon mean? | *To think or believe | To run | To buy | To sing
test: Lucy says, «Our team is a force to be reckoned with.» What does that suggest? | The team is far away | The team is hungry | *The team is strong and must be taken seriously | The team has lost the ball
exam: Ella asks, «Does reckon always mean the same thing?» What is the answer? | *No, it can mean think, estimate, expect or deal with, so check the context | Yes, it always means to sleep | Yes, it always means to count | No, it has no meaning at all
